  "body": "`site/content/guide/automate/mcp.md` (\"Setting up an MCP client\") gives a generic entry (`{\"command\": \"camcraft-cli\", \"args\": [\"mcp\"]}`) and says \"see your client's documentation\". `ai.md` (\"Connect an external agent\") is similar. A user who wants to use CamCraft from an AI client still has to work out three things:\n\n1. **The exact setup per client**, for example:\n   - Claude Code: `claude mcp add camcraft -s user -- <path to camcraft-cli> mcp` (then start a new session),\n   - Claude Desktop: the `mcpServers` entry and where its config file is on each OS,\n   - Codex: the `[mcp_servers.camcraft]` table in `~/.codex/config.toml`,\n   - Cursor: its MCP settings entry.\n   Each with the attached form (`mcp --connect 127.0.0.1:<port>` after `camcraft-ui --mcp-port <port>`).\n2. **Where `camcraft-cli` is after an install on each OS** - it is not on the PATH. On macOS it is `/Applications/CamCraft.app/Contents/MacOS/camcraft-cli`. Give the full path in the client entry, or a symlink once #3028 is fixed; a copy of the binary outside its folder cannot post (no posts folder).\n3. **A first check that it works:** a short prompt that makes the agent list the tools (or call `list_operation_types`), and what the common failures look like (wrong path, no posts folder, a file path the server refuses).\n\nFound while setting up the CamCraft MCP server in Claude Code on macOS (2026-11-20).\n\nDone means: `mcp.md` has a per-client table (command or config snippet per client, headless and attached) and a per-OS path table, `ai.md` links to it instead of repeating it, and both pages end with the first check. Steps are checked against v0.24.0 or newer.\n",
